## Decoding the question
The question is **evaluative and applied**. "**Can men be feminists?**" demands a **position**, argued both ways. "**Critically evaluate men's feminism**" names three lenses to use — **Connell's theory**, **pro-feminist men's movements** and the **manosphere** (the anti-feminist backlash) — so each needs its own heading. "**What role can men play … in Pakistan**" asks for **practical** roles, grounded in Pakistani institutions and examples. Men's feminism has **never been asked** in either part of the paper — a blind spot.

## The model answer

### 1. Introduction

#### i) Definition
**Men's feminism** (or **pro-feminism**) is men's support for gender equality, combined with the study of how **patriarchy shapes men** — the masculinities it rewards and the harm it does. It aims to change both men and the male-run institutions of society.

#### ii) The argument
Men **can** be feminists — as **allies accountable to women**, not as leaders who displace them. **R. W. Connell**'s theory shows why men's change is necessary: patriarchy is sustained by a hegemonic ideal of manhood from which all men draw a "**dividend**". Pro-feminist men's movements prove that men can reject that dividend; the online **manosphere** proves how fiercely others defend it. In Pakistan, where fathers, brothers, husbands and elders make the key decisions in women's lives, men's role is **decisive**.

### 2. What men's feminism is

#### i) Two strands

##### (a) Political support
Men who back women's rights to education, work, safety, property and political voice, and who challenge sexism among other men.

##### (b) The study of men
**Masculinity studies** — research on how manhood is made, ranked and enforced, and how it harms women and men alike.

#### ii) Its place in the syllabus
It sits beside liberal, radical, Marxist, psychoanalytic and postmodern feminism as a school in its own right, because it answers a question the others raised: if gender is socially constructed, **men's gender** can be reconstructed too.

### 3. A long history

#### i) Western allies
The French philosopher **Condorcet** argued for women's citizenship in ==1790==; **Frederick Douglass**, the only African-American to sign the Declaration of Sentiments, supported the vote resolution at **Seneca Falls** (==1848==); and **John Stuart Mill** published *The Subjection of Women* in ==1869==.

#### ii) South Asian and Muslim reformers
**Raja Ram Mohan Roy** campaigned against *sati*, banned in ==1829==; **Sayyid Mumtaz Ali** of Lahore wrote ***Huquq-un-Niswan*** (==1898==); **Qasim Amin** in Egypt wrote on women's liberation (==1899==); **Sheikh Abdullah** ("Papa Mian") opened a girls' school at Aligarh in ==1906==; and **Muhammad Ali Jinnah** told students in ==1944== that no nation can rise unless its women stand beside its men.

#### iii) The lesson
Men's support for women's rights is **not a Western novelty** — it has deep local roots.

### 4. Connell: the theory behind men's feminism

#### i) Hegemonic masculinity
In *Gender and Power* (==1987==) and *Masculinities* (==1995==), **R. W. Connell** described **hegemonic masculinity** — the honoured, dominant ideal of manhood (tough, heterosexual, the provider, in control) that legitimises men's power over women.

#### ii) A hierarchy of masculinities

##### (a) Complicit
Most men do not match the ideal but benefit from it.

##### (b) Marginalised and subordinated
Men lowered by class or ethnicity, and men called "unmanly" — gay or effeminate men — are ranked below.

#### iii) The patriarchal dividend
All men gain a **patriarchal dividend** — advantages in pay, authority, safety and respect — from women's subordination, whether or not they seek it. Men's feminism therefore asks men to **give up a dividend**, which explains both its moral force and its difficulty.

::: fig pyramid | Connell's hierarchy of masculinities — what men's feminism asks men to challenge.
Hegemonic: the honoured ideal — tough, dominant, provider
Complicit: draw the patriarchal dividend without matching the ideal
Marginalised: lowered by class, ethnicity or race
Subordinated: called "unmanly" — gay or effeminate men
:::

### 5. What patriarchy costs men

#### i) The evidence
Men live **shorter lives**, have higher **suicide** rates, are most of the victims of **homicide** and carry the **provider burden**. **bell hooks** (*The Will to Change*, ==2004==) argued that patriarchy wounds men emotionally by forbidding tenderness.

#### ii) In Pakistan
The provider burden means dowries, debt and migration for work; the saying "*mard ko dard nahi hota*" (a man feels no pain) teaches boys to hide feelings; and distant fatherhood is the norm.

#### iii) Why it matters
These costs give men **their own reason** to change — but men's feminism insists that they do not cancel the much greater harm patriarchy does to women.

### 6. The men's movements: four directions

#### i) Pro-feminist
Organisations such as the US **National Organization for Men Against Sexism** support women's rights and challenge male privilege.

#### ii) Men's liberation
From the 1970s, groups focused on the **costs of the male role** — emotional repression and overwork.

#### iii) Mythopoetic
**Robert Bly**'s *Iron John* (==1990==) urged men to recover a "deep masculinity" through ritual — criticised for romanticising manhood.

#### iv) Men's rights
An **anti-feminist** stream claiming men are now the victims — over custody, divorce and "false accusations". **Michael Messner** (==1997==) mapped these movements by how far they recognise **privilege**, **costs** and **differences** among men.

::: fig spectrum | Men's movements, from pro-feminist to anti-feminist.
@left Pro-feminist
@right Anti-feminist
10: Pro-feminist — challenge privilege
35: Men's liberation — costs of the male role
60: Mythopoetic — recover "deep masculinity"
90: Men's rights and the manosphere — men as victims
:::

### 7. Pro-feminist campaigns

#### i) White Ribbon
After the **Montreal massacre** of ==6 December 1989==, in which 14 women were killed, Canadian men launched the **White Ribbon Campaign** (==1991==), pledging never to commit, excuse or remain silent about violence against women.

#### ii) The UN and global networks
The **Cairo** population conference (==1994==) stressed male responsibility; **MenEngage** (==2004==) linked organisations worldwide; and UN Women's **HeForShe** campaign (==2014==) invited men to commit to equality.

### 8. The manosphere: the backlash online

#### i) What it is
The **manosphere** is a network of online communities and influencers who present feminism as an attack on men, promote dominance over women and blame women for men's problems.

#### ii) Why it appeals

##### (a) Aggrieved entitlement
**Michael Kimmel** describes "**aggrieved entitlement**" — the anger of men who feel promised a status they no longer receive.

##### (b) Economic insecurity
Unemployment and the loss of the breadwinner role make young men receptive to messages that blame women.

#### iii) Its effects
It spreads **misogyny**, normalises online abuse of women journalists, politicians and activists, and pushes some young men towards hostility to equality — the clearest evidence that **masculinity is contested**, and that feminism needs men who argue back.

### 9. The debate: can men be feminists?

#### i) Yes
bell hooks argued that feminism is a movement against **sexism**, not against men, and so is open to all who oppose sexism.

#### ii) Yes, but as allies
Many feminists prefer "**pro-feminist**" or "**ally**": men can support the movement but should not **lead** it, speak over women or claim credit.

#### iii) Caution
Critics warn of men joining for **image**, diverting attention and funds, and of men's groups sliding into men's rights.

#### iv) My position
Men can be feminists in the sense that matters — committed to equality and **accountable to women's organisations** — while recognising that they still hold the dividend they oppose.

### 10. Pakistan: why men's role is decisive

#### i) Men hold the decisions
Whether a girl goes to secondary school, whom and when she marries, whether she may work, vote or inherit — these are usually decided by **fathers, brothers, husbands and elders**. Any strategy that ignores men leaves the gatekeepers untouched.

#### ii) Men's violence
Honour killing, domestic violence and harassment are overwhelmingly committed by men, often to defend ***ghairat*** — so violence prevention must work with men's ideas of honour.

#### iii) Local models
The Islamabad NGO **Rozan** has worked since the late 1990s with **boys, young men and police** on gender, emotional health and violence; **Ziauddin Yousafzai**, father of Malala, built a girls' school in Swat and wrote *Let Her Fly* (==2018==).

### 11. What men can do in Pakistan

#### i) In the family
Send daughters to school, refuse child marriage, give sisters their **inheritance**, share care work, and reject violence.

#### ii) In institutions
Male legislators can sponsor and vote for pro-women laws; police officers can register FIRs without "compromise"; employers can enforce the **workplace harassment law 2010**; teachers can challenge stereotypes in textbooks.

#### iii) In religion and culture
Male scholars can show that Islam forbids forced marriage and guarantees women's inheritance and consent; writers and actors can model **caring masculinities**; influencers can answer the manosphere online.

### 12. Conclusion
Men can be feminists — not as leaders of women's struggle, but as **accountable allies** who challenge the masculinity that sustains patriarchy. Connell's theory explains why that change is necessary, the pro-feminist movements show it is possible, and the manosphere shows how strongly the dividend is defended. In Pakistan, where men stand at every gate of a woman's life, changing men is not an optional extra to feminism: it is one of its **central tasks**.

## Why this answer scores
- Takes a **clear position** ("yes, as accountable allies") and argues both sides.
- Uses all **three named lenses** — Connell, pro-feminist movements, the manosphere — under separate headings.
- Shows **local roots** (Roy, Mumtaz Ali, Papa Mian, Jinnah), answering the "Western" charge.
- Gives **practical roles** for Pakistani men in the family, institutions and religion.
- A **pyramid** and a **spectrum** figure organise the theory and the movements.
